const E=(title,body,principle,exercise,q,options,correct,explanation)=>L(title,body+'<p>'+principle+'</p>',principle,exercise,q,options,correct,explanation);
const additions=[
{id:'labsetup',title:'Build a safe cyber lab',subtitle:'Plan isolated VMs, choose current images, and verify your practice boundaries.',stage:0,icon:'lab',time:'90 min',skills:['Virtual machines','Kali','Isolation'],sources:['kali','juice','linux'],lessons:[
E('Virtual machines and the lab boundary','A virtual machine is a guest operating system running on a host through a hypervisor. Pick a supported hypervisor and image for your CPU architecture, memory, disk, and host OS. Kali’s current documentation includes several platforms; a historical VirtualBox 6.0 walkthrough is not a current compatibility guide. For Apple Silicon, check ARM guest support instead of assuming an x86 image will work.<p>Design the network before importing targets. An internal virtual network can join lab guests without exposing them to the host LAN. Host-only networking includes the host; NAT may allow outbound access; bridged mode puts a guest on the physical network. Read your hypervisor’s exact behavior. A snapshot helps restore a guest but is not a network-isolation control.</p>','A vulnerable target should have no route to an unintended network. Validate both permitted connectivity and the connections that should fail.','Draw host, learner VM, and target VM. Label each network adapter and permitted flow. Record how you will verify that the target is not exposed.','Which configuration choice directly concerns network exposure?',['The desktop wallpaper','The VM adapter’s network mode and routing','The name of the snapshot'],1,'Adapter mode and routing determine who can reach the guest; snapshots serve a different purpose.'),
E('Kali, Windows, and vulnerable targets','Kali is a collection of security tools, not a substitute for understanding operating systems. Obtain it from the official project, check the publisher’s verification instructions, and use the documentation matching your setup. Keep the learner machine maintained. Take a clean snapshot and record image provenance and versions.<p>Use supported Windows evaluation or licensed images when Windows study is needed. Deliberately vulnerable targets such as Juice Shop, DVWA, or Metasploitable belong in an isolated lab; some are legacy teaching artifacts. Disable unnecessary shared folders and clipboard integration. Keep personal accounts, real credentials, and production data out of target machines. Do not forward a vulnerable service through your home router.</p>','Treat the lab as a small system you must administer: inventory, maintenance, containment, evidence, and cleanup all matter.','Create a lab manifest: image source, license, architecture, purpose, network mode, snapshot, and cleanup owner. A diagram-only plan is acceptable before installation.','Is installing Kali enough to establish a secure practice environment?',['Yes, its tools handle every boundary','No; guest isolation, target selection, and scope still need configuration','Only if the VM runs full screen'],1,'Tool installation does not establish authorization, isolation, or safe handling of a vulnerable target.'),
E('Preflight, terminal fluency, and cleanup','Before an exercise, confirm the target belongs to the lab, check network settings, note the current user and directory, and record what you intend to observe. Practice harmless file operations in a disposable directory. Learn what each command reads, changes, or sends before using it. Use documentation for package management and network settings rather than pasting old tutorials blindly.<p>Afterward, preserve only the synthetic evidence you need, remove temporary access or files, restore a known state where appropriate, and verify the environment is quiet. Ten small challenges in the Field guide develop this habit. The browser terminal is a simulation: it teaches navigation and reading, and never runs commands on your computer.</p>','A repeatable exercise has a known starting state, an observable result, and a verified cleanup step.','Complete the simulated terminal challenge, then write a preflight and cleanup checklist for your proposed VM lab.','What belongs in a useful preflight check?',['Scope, target identity, network isolation, and intended actions','Only the tool’s logo','A promise to fix mistakes afterward'],0,'Preflight checks establish boundaries before an action has effects.') ]},
{id:'windows',title:'Windows, hosts & legacy risk',subtitle:'Understand Windows identities, services, evidence, and historical vulnerabilities.',stage:0,icon:'layers',time:'75 min',skills:['Windows','UAC','Event logs'],sources:['windows','response','attack'],lessons:[
E('Accounts, services, registry, and events','Windows security spans local and domain identities, access tokens, file permissions, services, scheduled tasks, and policy. The Registry stores configuration used by the OS and applications; a Registry entry is not automatically malicious. Understand the normal purpose, owner, permissions, and change history of an artifact before judging it.<p>Read event logs in context. Event identifiers are meaningful with their provider, channel, audit configuration, and OS version. A missing event may mean logging was not enabled or retained. A service account with broad privileges creates a larger consequence if its process is compromised. Learn normal administration before attempting to assess abnormal behavior.</p>','Separate identity, permission, execution, and evidence. A process name alone does not establish that a program is trustworthy.','Draw a fictional Windows service’s identity and permissions. List the logs and configuration records you would want to investigate a suspicious change.','Why might an expected Windows event be absent?',['The activity definitely never happened','Audit configuration or retention may not capture it','Windows does not generate security logs'],1,'Visibility depends on configuration, retention, and collection as well as the activity itself.'),
E('Privilege, UAC, and hardening','User Account Control helps manage elevation and administrative operations. It is one component of a larger design involving user rights, application control, patching, endpoint protection, and least privilege. Distinguish being a member of an administrative group from the privileges of a particular process token.<p>Assess permissions without weakening the system to make a demonstration work. Review service configuration, writable locations, approved software, and unnecessary administrator access through documented administration tools. An assessment should connect a permission weakness to impact and remediation. UAC bypass and privilege-escalation topics here are studied through trust boundaries and detection, not a bypass recipe.</p>','Verify the effective authority of the process and the resource it wants to access. Keep security controls enabled during normal practice.','Create a hardening review for a fictional workstation: account rights, updates, application control, encryption, logging, and recovery.','Does hiding a window reduce the privileges of a process?',['No; UI appearance and process authority are different','Yes, invisible windows cannot act','Only on older Windows versions'],0,'Authority comes from identities, tokens, and permissions, not whether a console window is visible.'),
E('EternalBlue, DoublePulsar, and historical case study','Historical curricula may demonstrate Windows 7-era SMB exploitation such as EternalBlue and a post-compromise implant such as DoublePulsar. These are distinct concepts: a vulnerability enables a failure; an implant is code or capability introduced after compromise. Study the official advisory, affected configurations, patch history, and observable consequences.<p>The learning goal is to connect unsupported software, exposed services, patch validation, segmentation, and incident response. A product banner is not proof of susceptibility, and the absence of a familiar exploit signature is not proof of safety. Never make an outdated VM internet-accessible to reproduce an old demonstration. Modern Windows assessment requires current documentation and supported test environments.</p>','Use historical incidents to learn enduring control failures. Do not treat a legacy exploit demonstration as a current assessment method.','Write a historical vulnerability brief with affected component, missing control, exposure, mitigation, and evidence needed to validate a fix.','Which distinction is accurate?',['An exploit and a post-compromise implant serve different roles','Every SMB server is vulnerable to the same exploit','A version banner proves successful exploitation'],0,'Different stages and artifacts must be evaluated separately using applicability and evidence.') ]},
{id:'recon',title:'Reconnaissance & enumeration',subtitle:'Learn passive research, DNS, routing, and authorized service inventories.',stage:1,icon:'globe',time:'75 min',skills:['DNS','OSINT','Enumeration'],sources:['nmap','wireshark','attack'],lessons:[
E('Passive research and active footprinting','Passive research uses existing information such as published documentation, certificate records, or third-party indexes. Active discovery sends traffic to an assessed system. The distinction helps reason about permissions, privacy, freshness, and the possibility of operational impact. A third-party query may still create a record or disclose your interest.<p>For a lab, start with a fictional asset inventory. DNS names, certificate names, and search results can suggest assets but do not prove current ownership or authorization. Tools such as theHarvester, Amass, DNSDumpster, Shodan, and Censys collect different kinds of evidence. Confirm provenance and timestamps. Avoid collecting personal information unless it is necessary and specifically authorized.</p>','Discovery results are leads. Ownership, scope, and current configuration require verification.','Build a passive-research worksheet for a fictional organization. Include source, timestamp, hypothesis, verification step, and privacy limit.','A certificate record contains a hostname. Does that authorize testing it?',['Yes, certificate records are public','No; it is a lead that still needs ownership and scope checks','Only if it includes a wildcard'],1,'Public metadata does not grant testing permission or establish present ownership.'),
E('DNS, ping, traceroute, and local discovery','DNS records describe parts of a naming configuration. Address, alias, and mail records answer different questions; cached and historical data may differ from current state. WHOIS or RDAP offers registration information that may be redacted and should not be treated as a person’s verified identity.<p>Ping tests a particular kind of reachability when allowed. Traceroute estimates a path using protocol behavior; missing responses can reflect filtering, rate limits, or device policy. Layer-two discovery tools such as Netdiscover and some Bettercap functions observe a local segment, not the entire internet. NAT, VPNs, asymmetric routes, and multiple interfaces complicate interpretation.</p>','An unanswered probe is not the same as an absent host. Report the observation and plausible explanations separately.','Interpret a synthetic route with an intermediate nonresponse and a responsive destination. Explain why the missing hop does not prove a broken path.','What can a missing traceroute response mean?',['Filtering or rate limiting, among other possibilities','The device definitely does not exist','The destination is automatically compromised'],0,'Network devices do not have to answer every diagnostic probe.'),
E('From discovery to a scoped inventory','Enumeration adds detail to an asset: listening service, protocol, version evidence, authentication surface, and trust relationship. Record how each fact was obtained and how confident you are. A server may expose several services, and proxies can obscure what runs behind them.<p>Website technology-identification and content-discovery tools such as WhatWeb and DIRB may send many requests. Rate, scope, account permissions, and application stability matter. Begin in a provided lab, keep a request budget, and stop when the scope is unclear. Convert results into an inventory that supports a next decision: verify a configuration, review access, or investigate a component.</p>','A useful inventory connects observations to owners and decisions, rather than collecting names without context.','Create a five-column fictional inventory: asset, observed service, evidence, confidence, and authorized next step.','Which is the best enumeration result?',['A screenshot with no target or time','A scoped observation with source, timestamp, confidence, and owner','A long list of unverified internet addresses'],1,'An accountable, evidenced inventory is actionable and reproducible.') ]},
{id:'assessment',title:'Scanning & vulnerability assessment',subtitle:'Interpret Nmap, assess findings, and understand defensive visibility.',stage:1,icon:'search',time:'90 min',skills:['Nmap','Vulnerabilities','IDS/IPS'],sources:['nmap','kev','d3fend'],lessons:[
E('Read scan output before adding options','A scan is an observation from a particular vantage point using particular probes. Nmap distinguishes states such as open, closed, and filtered; these are interpretations of responses, not permanent properties independent of the network. A listening port may expose an unexpected service, and a filter can change what the scanner sees.<p>Service and OS identification are estimates supported by response characteristics. Document target authorization, scan scope, source location, time, traffic budget, and tool version. Start with the smallest justified test in an isolated lab. If a result is surprising, compare a packet capture or known configuration rather than immediately increasing scan intensity.</p>','Report what was observed, how it was observed, and the limits of the observation.','Review the synthetic scan in the Field guide. Separate a listening service, a refused connection, and a filtered observation.','What does a filtered result generally indicate?',['The scanner could not determine the port state because filtering or related behavior obscured it','A confirmed vulnerable application','A guarantee that no service exists'],0,'The result describes what the scan could establish from that vantage point.'),
E('Validate and prioritize a vulnerability','A scanner finding is a hypothesis supported by evidence. Match the product, version, configuration, and affected feature to an official advisory. Authenticated scans can see different information from unauthenticated ones. Tools such as Nessus, OpenVAS, Nikto, and OpenSCAP address different questions, from exposure to configuration baselines.<p>SearchSploit indexes public exploit references; Shodan indexes observed internet services. Neither makes a finding applicable or authorizes testing. Do not run a downloaded exploit to compensate for missing evidence. Prioritize using exposure, exploitation evidence, asset impact, mitigations, and a practical remediation plan. Record a safe verification method and a retest.</p>','A version string or scanner label is a starting point, not a confirmed compromise.','Create a fictional finding with advisory reference, applicability checks, impact, owner, remediation, and retest criteria.','What should happen before calling a scanner finding confirmed?',['Verify applicability and the supporting evidence','Run every exploit found online','Copy its severity into a report without review'],0,'Configuration and applicability checks reduce false positives and unsafe testing.'),
E('Firewalls, IDS/IPS, honeypots, and evasion limits','A firewall enforces traffic policy. An IDS observes and alerts; an IPS can also interrupt matching traffic. Honeypots and deception systems provide intentionally instrumented resources. Detection depends on placement, telemetry, encryption, normalization, rules, and response processes; no one control guarantees complete visibility.<p>Decoys, spoofing, packet fragmentation, and altered probe timing appear in offensive course outlines. Here they are examined as detection and attribution limitations. Fragment handling and correlation matter because devices can reconstruct or interpret traffic differently. Compare synthetic sensor records and document blind spots. Avoid presenting a setting as a reliable firewall or IDS bypass, and do not give students concealment exercises on live systems.</p>','A control assessment should make detection gaps observable and help improve defenses, rather than reward hiding from them.','Using a fictional network diagram, choose sensor locations and explain how encrypted traffic or fragmented observations could limit your conclusions.','Why is one sensor’s silence insufficient to declare an assessment undetected?',['It may have a visibility or collection gap','Silence always proves that every control was bypassed','Honeypots disable all logs'],0,'Absence of an alert must be interpreted against the sensor’s coverage and configuration.') ]},
{id:'wireless',title:'Wireless security & rogue networks',subtitle:'Understand Wi-Fi authentication, passive evidence, and rogue access-point risks.',stage:2,icon:'network',time:'75 min',skills:['Wi-Fi','WPA2/WPA3','Rogue APs'],sources:['wifi','aircrack','identity'],lessons:[
E('Wi-Fi authentication and handshake concepts','A wireless network shares a radio medium; physical proximity does not imply permission to observe or interfere with another network. Understand the difference between network names, access-point identifiers, channels, association, and the authentication method. WPA/WPA2 shared-password networks and enterprise authentication establish trust differently; current designs may use WPA3.<p>A handshake is a protocol exchange used to establish cryptographic state. In some legacy or shared-secret configurations, an authorized capture can support offline password-guessing analysis. A captured exchange is not itself a plaintext password. Good study connects the protocol to secret strength, configuration, client behavior, and migration guidance.</p>','Network names are labels, not proof of identity. Protocol design and configuration determine the trust being established.','Draw a conceptual client-to-AP authentication exchange and identify what each party must trust. Use a diagram or provided sample capture, not nearby networks.','Does a captured handshake necessarily contain the Wi-Fi password in plaintext?',['Yes','No; it records a protocol exchange, with implications depending on the configuration','Only if the SSID is short'],1,'Protocol evidence and recovered credentials are different; the details depend on authentication design.'),
E('Monitor mode, offline audits, and availability','Monitor mode allows suitable hardware and drivers to observe certain wireless frames without normal client association. Support varies, including guest USB passthrough and host restrictions. A course’s minimum RAM or adapter recommendation is not a universal current hardware requirement.<p>Aircrack-ng, Hashcat, and John the Ripper are examples of audit tooling; their use requires authorized evidence and carefully bounded compute. Deauthentication concerns management traffic and can disrupt connectivity. This site explains the availability risk and protective controls without instructions to disconnect clients, capture real credentials, or interfere with third-party radio networks.</p>','Use provided sample data for protocol study. Treat availability and radio interference as part of scope, even when the exercise is called a lab.','Write an authorization checklist for a fictional wireless assessment: ownership, client consent, permitted observations, location, stop conditions, and data disposal.','What is a direct risk of a deauthentication exercise?',['Disrupting legitimate connectivity','Increasing disk capacity','Automatically patching the access point'],0,'Availability impacts require explicit consideration and controlled boundaries.'),
E('Evil twins, captive portals, and client trust','An evil twin is a deceptive access point impersonating a trusted network. A captive portal is an access workflow and is not automatically malicious. Fake portals and lookalike SSIDs can pressure users to disclose credentials or accept unsafe trust changes.<p>Defensive study examines how a client selects a network, validates enterprise authentication certificates, handles portal redirects, and warns about trust. Tools such as Airgeddon and Bettercap appear in assessment ecosystems, but a tool name does not establish legitimacy. Use screenshots and fictional messages to analyze cues; do not create credential-harvesting services. Protect users through configuration, clear onboarding, reporting channels, and phishing-resistant authentication.</p>','A familiar SSID or branded page is not sufficient evidence that a network or login request is trustworthy.','Compare two fictional onboarding messages. Identify the one that asks a user to ignore a certificate warning and explain the appropriate response.','A familiar Wi-Fi name appears with an unexpected request for your email password. What should you do?',['Enter it because the name matches','Stop, verify through the institution’s trusted channel, and report the request','Disable all certificate checks'],1,'Names and branding can be copied. Verify through a separate trusted route.') ]},
{id:'mitm',title:'Traffic interception & trust',subtitle:'Understand ARP, proxies, DNS redirection, and evidence without collecting secrets.',stage:1,icon:'network',time:'75 min',skills:['ARP','TLS','Traffic analysis'],sources:['wireshark','burp','ietf'],lessons:[
E('ARP spoofing and the local trust boundary','ARP helps a host associate an IPv4 address with a local link-layer address. Its ordinary operation does not provide a strong identity guarantee. Manipulated neighbor information can redirect local traffic, causing interruption or placing an intermediary on a path. The exact effect depends on topology, switch protections, endpoint state, and routing.<p>Learn the difference between controlling a path and reading encrypted content. An intermediary normally cannot decrypt properly validated TLS merely by forwarding packets. Compare a synthetic neighbor-table change with a benign gateway replacement. Defensive controls can include segmentation, switch protections where supported, secure protocol use, and investigation of inconsistent observations.</p>','Network-path control and application confidentiality are separate questions. Preserve TLS validation and distinguish evidence from assumptions.','Draw an ordinary local packet path and a redirected path. Mark which observations would help distinguish a network change from malicious redirection.','Does ARP redirection alone guarantee decryption of correctly validated HTTPS?',['No','Yes, TLS no longer matters','Only if the router has a short name'],0,'TLS protects application traffic independently of ordinary local address resolution.'),
E('Proxies, certificates, and content manipulation','An explicit testing proxy sits between a lab browser and a training application. For HTTPS inspection, a browser must trust the proxy’s testing certificate; that is a significant trust change. Use a dedicated lab browser profile, understand the trust store, and remove temporary trust afterward. Do not apply a lab certificate to everyday browsing without a clear, authorized need.<p>DNS redirection and captive portals can change where users go, while HTML or image manipulation affects content integrity when protections fail or an intermediary is trusted. MITMf/Python 2 examples in older courses are historical; do not rebuild obsolete stacks as a prerequisite. Study modern protocol behavior and a maintained testing proxy instead.</p>','Certificate trust changes who may read or modify protected traffic. Keep temporary testing trust confined to the lab.','Write a lab-proxy lifecycle: isolated profile, approved target, certificate scope, observation, and trust cleanup.','Why use a separate browser profile for a testing proxy certificate?',['To limit the reach of temporary trust changes','To bypass every site’s authentication','To hide the test from the owner'],0,'A separate profile reduces accidental exposure of everyday browsing to testing trust.'),
E('Packet sniffing, privacy, and evidence','Packet analysis can answer questions about timing, connection state, protocol use, and unexpected destinations. It can also capture sensitive information. Define the minimum needed evidence, collection window, access permissions, retention, and sanitization before recording traffic.<p>Do not reproduce password-sniffing, fake-login, or keylogging demonstrations against real users. Use synthetic captures to identify a plaintext protocol and recommend encrypted alternatives. Correlate packet timestamps with server and endpoint logs, noting time zones and capture gaps. An encrypted flow still exposes some metadata; metadata should also be handled with care.</p>','Collect to answer a question, and protect the resulting evidence. More traffic is not always more insight.','Create an evidence plan for a fictional intermittent login issue. State what you will capture, what you will exclude, and when you will delete it.','Which capture plan best respects data minimization?',['Record everyone’s traffic indefinitely','Capture a defined test flow for a limited window and protect the evidence','Upload all packets publicly before reviewing them'],1,'A defined question and bounded collection reduce unnecessary exposure.') ]},
{id:'webdepth',title:'Web testing, one weakness at a time',subtitle:'Connect Burp and DevTools to injection, authorization, and file-handling controls.',stage:1,icon:'code',time:'120 min',skills:['Burp Suite','SQLi/XSS','Access control'],sources:['burp','webtopics','zap','juice'],lessons:[
E('DevTools, Burp Suite, and an assessment method','Browser DevTools shows requests, responses, storage, and client behavior. Burp Suite and ZAP provide proxy-based workflows for inspecting a training application. Begin with one normal request, identify its inputs and expected authorization, and formulate one testable hypothesis. Keep a record of the original request and the observation.<p>Map authentication, sessions, objects, and state-changing actions. Technology identification, content discovery, and scanners may support the map, but manual reasoning establishes why a behavior matters. Use a provider-hosted lab or an isolated app such as Juice Shop or DVWA. Configure interception and certificate trust only for the lab. A tool tutorial is not permission to assess arbitrary websites.</p>','Use a repeatable method: observe normal behavior, identify a trust boundary, test one assumption, explain impact, propose a fix, and retest.','In a hosted Academy beginner lab, identify method, path, parameters, cookies, and the server-side authorization decision. Write an evidence-only report without solution secrets.','Which question should guide an individual web test?',['Which trust assumption am I checking?','How many tools can I launch at once?','Can I hide my requests from the application owner?'],0,'A clear hypothesis makes results explainable and keeps testing focused.'),
E('SQL, command, HTML, and script injection','Injection occurs when untrusted data is interpreted in a more powerful context. SQL injection crosses a database-query boundary; command injection crosses an OS-command boundary. Prefer parameterized queries for SQL, and avoid invoking a shell where a narrowly scoped library API can do the work. Validation alone is not a substitute for separating data from instructions.<p>HTML injection changes document structure. XSS executes script in a browser context and can be reflected, stored, or DOM-based. Use context-appropriate output encoding, safe DOM APIs, and careful sanitization when HTML is intentionally allowed. HTML-character encoding is not interchangeable across JavaScript, CSS, URL, and HTML contexts. A Content Security Policy is an additional layer, not a universal fix.</p>','For each weakness, identify source, interpretation context, impact, root-cause mitigation, and a regression test.','Choose SQL injection and XSS beginner topics from the Academy. Compare their trust boundaries and write two prevention-oriented test cases.','Which statement about encoding is accurate?',['One encoding function is safe in every context','The required encoding depends on the output context','Base64 removes all injection risks'],1,'Different interpreters have different syntax; prevention must match the context.'),
E('Access control, files, and adjacent web risks','Broken access control lets a user perform an action or access an object they should not. Enforce object and action checks on the server. File upload introduces parsing, storage, naming, access, and execution risks; path traversal lets an input escape an intended file boundary. Store uploads outside executable paths and apply permissions, content rules, size limits, and download authorization.<p>Study CSRF, SSRF, authentication logic, and business-logic weaknesses as related but distinct topics. CSRF misuses an authenticated browser’s ability to send actions; SSRF misuses a server’s ability to reach other resources. For each, ask which actor supplies data and which component exercises authority. Use the relevant maintained Academy labs for practice, then explain the fix in your own words.</p>','A hidden control or a filename check does not establish authorization. Verify the complete operation and the effective boundary.','Threat-model a project’s upload/download feature. Include ownership checks, canonical paths, safe storage, size limits, and a denied-access test.','Where must a file download’s ownership check happen?',['Only in a hidden UI button','On the server that serves the protected object','Only in the file extension'],1,'The server must enforce access regardless of how the request is generated.') ]},
{id:'systems',title:'System assessment & post-access risk',subtitle:'Study shells, frameworks, persistence, and cleanup through defensive evidence.',stage:1,icon:'terminal',time:'90 min',skills:['System assessment','Sockets','Post-access risk'],sources:['metasploit','windows','attack','python'],lessons:[
E('Frameworks, exploit modules, and evidence','An assessment framework organizes components and workflows; it does not remove the need for scope, applicability, safety review, and reporting. Distinguish a vulnerability check, exploit behavior, payload, session, and post-access activity. Metasploit is one ecosystem where these terms appear.<p>Study module documentation and a fictional execution timeline rather than running unreviewed code. A successful session does not automatically establish the business impact, and a failed demonstration does not prove a weakness is absent. Historical Windows exploit examples should be discussed with their exact affected component and current defensive mitigations, rather than presented as a way to compromise any modern system.</p>','Evidence quality and safe validation matter more than the tool’s success message.','Read a framework’s component overview, then label a fictional assessment timeline with check, attempted action, observation, interpretation, and cleanup.','Does an exploit framework’s success message replace a finding’s evidence and impact analysis?',['No','Yes, all successful sessions prove equal impact','Only when a paid edition is used'],0,'A finding still needs scope, evidence, impact, limitations, remediation, and verification.'),
E('Reverse and bind shells; Python and C networking','A reverse connection is initiated from one side toward a listener; a bind-style service listens for an incoming connection. Shells attach command execution to a channel. Understanding direction, processes, identities, and network paths helps defenders interpret suspicious remote-control behavior.<p>Python and C can implement ordinary socket clients and servers. Learn address structures, byte buffers, connection lifecycle, timeouts, error handling, and resource cleanup through a harmless local echo design. The exercise sends a fixed synthetic message and receives it back; it does not invoke a command interpreter, access real data, hide a window, persist, or generate a remote-control executable. Packaging code into an EXE changes distribution, not authorization or trust.</p>','A network connection and an operating-system command channel are different capabilities. Constrain the program to the capability the learning goal needs.','Draw a loopback-only echo client/server. Describe address validation, a fixed message, a length limit, a timeout, and socket cleanup in Python or C pseudocode.','What makes the echo exercise different from a remote shell?',['It returns a bounded message without executing operating-system commands','It uses a different wallpaper','Every socket program is automatically a shell'],0,'The allowed behavior of the program, not just its transport, determines its authority.'),
E('Post-access activity: credentials, persistence, and cleanup','After unauthorized access, potential behaviors include credential access, privilege escalation, persistence, discovery, lateral movement, and collection. These are separate questions with different evidence. Meterpreter, Registry startup entries, UAC-related behavior, browser credential stores, and WLAN profiles appear in offensive curricula; study their security meaning and observables without extracting credentials or implementing persistence.<p>Correlate an unusual process with identity, parent process, network destination, configuration changes, and timestamps. Backdoors hidden in apparent images or other files highlight file-type deception; inspect untrusted artifacts in an appropriate analysis environment. Never preserve access as a classroom objective. A legitimate assessment has agreed stop conditions, evidence handling, revocation, cleanup, and validation by the owner.</p>','Post-access coverage here means concepts, detection, impact, and recovery. It is not a credential-extraction or persistence lab.','Build a synthetic incident timeline with an unexpected startup change and an outbound connection. Identify evidence to collect, access to revoke, and cleanup to verify.','Which activity belongs in the closeout of an authorized assessment?',['Leaving a hidden account for next time','Removing temporary access and validating cleanup with the owner','Publishing any recovered credentials'],1,'Assessment authority ends with the agreed scope; cleanup and validation are essential.') ]},
{id:'malware',title:'Malware analysis & executable behavior',subtitle:'Analyze files, memory, and behavioral evidence without building a malicious payload.',stage:2,icon:'search',time:'90 min',skills:['Static analysis','Memory','YARA'],sources:['ghidra','yara','memory','forensics'],lessons:[
E('Static, dynamic, and memory analysis','Static analysis studies a file without executing it: format, metadata, strings, imports, and disassembly can reveal hypotheses. Dynamic analysis observes execution in a purpose-built, isolated environment. Memory analysis studies captured process and system state. Each method has blind spots and can affect evidence.<p>Ghidra, PEStudio, YARA, Volatility, and sandbox platforms support different parts of this work. Begin with benign educational files and synthetic memory or event artifacts. A suspicious string is not proof of execution; a clean sandbox run is not proof of safety. Record provenance, hashes, analysis conditions, and confidence. Do not upload private samples to public services without authorization.</p>','No single analysis technique can prove a program harmless. Combine evidence and state the limits of what you observed.','Create an analysis plan for an unknown synthetic file: acquisition, hash, static questions, isolated dynamic questions, memory questions, and reporting.','What can a static string alone prove?',['That the text is present in the inspected representation, with context still needed','That the corresponding code definitely executed','That the file is harmless'],0,'Presence in a file is weaker evidence than a validated runtime observation.'),
E('C executables, sockets, and deceptive behavior','A compiled C program has entry points, libraries, memory operations, and platform APIs. Study address structures such as sockaddr_in, buffers, and API entry conventions through a benign echo application or a small documented sample. Clearing a buffer, compiling an executable, or showing an error dialog is not inherently malicious; intent and behavior depend on context.<p>Malware can combine ordinary APIs with unauthorized command execution, credential access, concealment, or persistence. Course topics about hiding a console or presenting a deceptive error are examined here as analysis cues. We do not construct a program that remotely executes commands, disguises itself to deceive users, or installs a backdoor. Explain capabilities, likely evidence, and defensive controls instead.</p>','Analyze what a binary can do and what evidence supports the claim. Do not infer harmlessness from its icon, extension, or user interface.','Review a fictional import list and process timeline. Separate file capabilities, observed behavior, and conclusions that need more evidence.','Does an ordinary-looking error dialog prove that a program did nothing else?',['Yes','No; UI appearance does not establish the program’s behavior','Only if the dialog uses a system icon'],1,'Background actions and permissions must be assessed independently of the visible interface.'),
E('Encoding, binary changes, and detection limits','Encoding changes representation; encryption protects data with a key; packing changes how executable content is stored or loaded. None guarantees that behavior is safe or invisible. Changing bytes can alter a file hash while leaving its operational behavior similar, so exact-hash detection alone has limitations.<p>Study defense evasion as a detection-engineering problem. Evaluate file provenance, process relationships, network activity, memory behavior, and relevant rule conditions. Hex editors and encoders are legitimate tools, but this site does not teach antivirus-bypass procedures or promise an “undetectable” payload. Use a harmless text marker to learn matching and false positives; changing a marker teaches rule scope without developing concealment.</p>','A defensible detection claims a measured scope and known limits. “Undetectable” is not a reliable security guarantee.','Design a text-pattern rule for a harmless marker. Describe one true match, one false positive, one missed representation, and how to improve the rule.','Why is exact-file-hash matching insufficient by itself?',['A changed representation can produce a different hash','All hashes are reversible','A matching hash proves that every program is malicious'],0,'Hash matching identifies an exact representation; behavior and other evidence need separate analysis.') ]},
{id:'credentialaudit',title:'Credential audits & account defense',subtitle:'Understand guessing, offline analysis, phishing, and recovery with synthetic examples.',stage:1,icon:'key',time:'75 min',skills:['Password auditing','Rate limits','Account recovery'],sources:['identity','hashcat','cs50'],lessons:[
E('Online guessing and offline auditing','Online guessing interacts with an authentication service and can affect users, logs, availability, and lockouts. Credential stuffing reuses exposed credential pairs; password spraying distributes guesses across accounts. Offline auditing tests guesses against authorized password-verification material without calling the login service. These behaviors have different evidence and risks.<p>Hashcat and John the Ripper are offline audit tools; Hydra and similar tools can automate online authentication attempts. Their presence does not provide authorization. Study cost, rate limiting, salts, work factors, MFA, and recovery protections using synthetic data. Do not import leaked credentials or test other people’s accounts.</p>','An audit must define data ownership, permitted actions, resource limits, handling, and disposal.','Compare the evidence and controls for online guessing, credential stuffing, spraying, and offline auditing in a four-row table.','Which activity sends attempts to a login service?',['Online guessing','Only offline hash verification','Reading a local policy document'],0,'Online attempts interact with the service and can have immediate operational effects.'),
E('A Python project: model rate limiting','Instead of a website brute-forcer, build a pure simulation of authentication decisions. Give a fictional account a list of timestamped synthetic attempts and evaluate a threshold or cooldown rule. The browser exercise also models this deterministically: it sends no requests and collects no credentials.<p>A simple global threshold can block legitimate users behind shared networks or create a denial-of-service opportunity. Evaluate per-account and context-aware controls, user experience, recovery, and monitoring. A model is not a deployable authentication system; document what it omits. Compare expected outcomes with actual simulated outcomes so the project demonstrates testing, not just a loop over guesses.</p>','Automation can teach the decision logic without targeting a real login page or handling a real password.','Write pseudocode accepting only a fixed array of synthetic attempts. Produce allowed, throttled, and review-needed outcomes; add a benign-user test and a reset-window test.','Which project stays entirely within the intended simulation?',['A local function processing predefined attempts without network access','A script submitting guesses to a public site','Importing a credential dump'],0,'A bounded local model isolates the learning goal from real accounts and services.'),
E('Phishing, social media, and account recovery','Social-media account compromise can involve phishing, reused credentials, unsafe recovery, malicious apps, or a compromised endpoint. A branded login page or a message from a familiar account is not proof that an authentication request is legitimate. Use a trusted app or a saved official address to verify a request independently.<p>Fake captive portals and lookalike login pages are studied here through fictional screenshots and decision exercises, not credential collection. Defensive controls include phishing-resistant authentication where available, unique passwords, session review, app-permission review, and a tested recovery route. Following compromise, preserve evidence, revoke unwanted sessions, repair recovery channels, and use the provider’s official support process.</p>','Account defense covers sign-in, sessions, connected apps, recovery, and user reporting together.','Create a fictional campus social-media recovery checklist. Include independent verification, session revocation, app permissions, and evidence handling.','A message says your account will be deleted unless you sign in through its link. What is the safer next step?',['Open the official app independently and check the account there','Enter the password immediately','Forward the message with your password'],0,'Independent navigation avoids treating a message’s link as a trusted authentication destination.') ]},
{id:'mobileot',title:'Mobile, IoT & AI systems',subtitle:'Understand specialist surfaces and where security meets privacy and physical safety.',stage:2,icon:'cloud',time:'90 min',skills:['Mobile','OT/IoT','ML security'],sources:['mobile','ot','genai'],lessons:[
E('Mobile application security','Mobile applications combine a client, device platform, remote APIs, local storage, and distribution channels. Review permissions, sensitive-data storage, transport, authentication, and server-side authorization. A hardened client cannot compensate for a vulnerable API.<p>OWASP MAS provides structured mobile guidance. MobSF can support static and dynamic assessment; Frida and Objection provide instrumentation workflows. Learn these tools through a purpose-built app you own or an approved training target. Instrumentation changes observations and may affect app behavior. Do not bypass third-party account controls, intercept real user data, or treat device possession as permission to access someone else’s accounts.</p>','Model the whole mobile system. Client, server, platform, and data-lifecycle controls have different responsibilities.','Threat-model a fictional campus mobile app with offline notes and a student-record API. Identify local and server-side controls separately.','Can hiding an API key in a mobile app replace server authorization?',['No','Yes, a compiled client is a secret store','Only if its icon is changed'],0,'Distributed clients cannot be trusted to enforce the server’s access policy.'),
E('IoT and operational technology','IoT devices can expose weak defaults, update problems, cloud dependencies, and long service lives. Operational technology can also control physical processes, where availability, safety, and deterministic operation are central. A scanning method acceptable on an IT lab may interrupt a fragile controller.<p>Use NIST OT guidance and relevant ISA/IEC 62443 resources to study asset inventories, zones, conduits, remote access, monitoring, and recovery. Work with process and safety owners. For a student project, analyze architecture diagrams or vendor-provided training material; do not actively probe a real industrial, medical, building-control, or campus safety system.</p>','Security decisions in cyber-physical systems must account for physical consequences and operational constraints.','Draw a fictional temperature-monitoring system with sensor, gateway, dashboard, and operator. Mark updates, identity, network boundaries, and a safe failure mode.','Why can ordinary IT scanning be unsuitable for OT?',['Some devices and processes can be disrupted, with physical consequences','OT systems never have networks','Industrial devices do not need authorization'],0,'Operational constraints and safety require specifically approved assessment methods.'),
E('ML pipelines, robotics, and synthetic identity','AI engineering includes data collection, labeling, training, evaluation, model storage, deployment, and monitoring. Security concerns include poisoned data, exposed artifacts, excessive tool permissions, unsafe output handling, and dependencies. Record data provenance, access rights, evaluation limits, and rollback plans.<p>PyTorch, TensorFlow, MLflow, Hugging Face, Weights & Biases, and managed ML platforms are adjacent engineering tools rather than cybersecurity qualifications by themselves. Robotics adds actuator authority and physical safety. Deepfake and synthetic-identity risks require verification processes that do not rely solely on convincing audio or video. Use fictional datasets and nonphysical simulations for student studies.</p>','Treat models and datasets as versioned assets with owners, permissions, provenance, and measured limitations.','Threat-model a fictional assistant that can recommend, but cannot execute, changes to a robotics simulator. Identify how a human reviews consequential actions.','What changes when an AI assistant can act on tools rather than only produce text?',['Its permissions and possible consequences become part of the security boundary','Nothing; generated text is always harmless','It no longer needs logging'],0,'Tool authority connects generated behavior to external effects and requires enforced boundaries.') ]},
{id:'architecture',title:'Security architecture & context',subtitle:'Connect history, layered defenses, Zero Trust, and emerging risks.',stage:0,icon:'layers',time:'75 min',skills:['OSI','Defense in depth','Zero Trust'],sources:['nist','ietf','windows','pqc'],lessons:[
E('How the field arrived here','Security evolved alongside wider connectivity: research networks, public web applications, personal devices, cloud services, software supply chains, and AI systems. Historical examples such as the Morris Worm, Stuxnet, WannaCry, and SolarWinds illustrate different failure modes: software propagation, cyber-physical impact, patch and legacy risk, and trust in a supplier.<p>Use historical incidents to compare the affected assets, entry assumptions, scope of harm, and changes to defensive practice. Avoid repeating dramatic breach figures without their original methodology and date. A trend is not a guaranteed forecast, and an old incident does not prove that every modern system is vulnerable in the same way.</p>','The useful historical question is what changed in the system’s trust assumptions and what controls would reduce similar risk today.','Choose one incident from official incident-response material. Write an asset–failure–impact–control timeline and distinguish confirmed facts from later commentary.','Which is the strongest use of a historical incident?',['Extract the failed assumption and compare current controls','Assume its exploit works against every present-day system','Repeat an unsourced cost statistic'],0,'Transferable reasoning is more useful than assuming the same exploit or impact remains applicable.'),
E('OSI and seven layers of defense','The OSI model separates physical, data-link, network, transport, session, presentation, and application responsibilities. It is a teaching model; real protocols can cross conceptual boundaries. TLS provides a secure channel above a reliable transport in common HTTPS stacks, and protocol placement is more nuanced than assigning every feature to one rigid box.<p>Defense in depth is a different kind of layering: human decisions, physical access, perimeter, network, endpoint, application, and data protections can reinforce each other. Map a control to what it actually does. A badge reader does not validate a web request, a firewall does not prove record ownership, and encryption does not make a dangerous command authorized.</p>','Protocol layers describe communication responsibilities. Defensive layers describe complementary ways to reduce risk; the two models are related but not interchangeable.','Map one college portal request across protocol responsibilities, then separately list human, physical, endpoint, network, application, and data controls.','Do OSI layers and defense-in-depth rings describe exactly the same model?',['No; they organize different aspects of a system','Yes, each has identical controls','Only when a service uses HTTPS'],0,'OSI is a communication reference model; defense in depth is a security strategy.'),
E('Zero Trust, constraints, and future planning','Zero Trust emphasizes explicit, context-aware access decisions and limited authority rather than assuming everything inside a perimeter is trustworthy. Identity, device state, segmentation, policy, telemetry, and review work together. It is an architectural approach, not a single product.<p>Security teams face limited time, legacy systems, expanding assets, alert fatigue, insider risk, and competing operational needs. Prioritize using evidence and consequences. AI, connected devices, deepfakes, robotics, and post-quantum migration add planning questions, but exact long-range forecasts remain uncertain. Start with inventories, owners, least privilege, recoverability, and a clear review process.</p>','A practical architecture explains assumptions, tradeoffs, failure modes, and how controls will be verified over time.','Design a one-page target architecture for a fictional engineering project. Include an access decision, a detection path, a recovery path, and one explicit residual risk.','Which statement best reflects Zero Trust?',['Internal location alone should not grant broad access','One vendor product eliminates all trust decisions','Everything on a campus LAN is inherently trusted'],0,'Location may inform policy but should not replace explicit access decisions.') ]},
{id:'professional',title:'Professional practice & research',subtitle:'Connect an engagement plan to standards, law, evidence, and a research path.',stage:2,icon:'compass',time:'90 min',skills:['Reporting','Standards','Research'],sources:['disclosure','nist','ssdf','certin','bns'],lessons:[
E('An engagement from scope to retest','A common teaching model describes reconnaissance, scanning, gaining access, maintaining access, and covering tracks. It describes attacker-oriented phases and is not a permission slip or a complete professional engagement plan. Authorized work also requires objectives, written scope, rules of engagement, communications, evidence handling, reporting, cleanup, and retesting.<p>Define stop conditions before any test: unexpected personal data, operational instability, an out-of-scope system, or a request exceeding the agreement. Bug-bounty scope and safe-harbor terms are program-specific; a platform listing does not authorize every action. Report minimal sufficient evidence privately. A mature closeout removes temporary access and verifies fixes without deleting evidence to hide activity.</p>','Professional assessment work must be accountable and reproducible. Preserving agreed evidence differs from an attacker trying to cover tracks.','Draft a fictional engagement plan with objectives, permissions, limits, stop conditions, reporting, cleanup, and retest.','Which activity is consistent with accountable authorized work?',['Keeping an agreed evidence trail and verifying cleanup','Deleting logs to hide assessment activity','Maintaining secret access after the engagement ends'],0,'A legitimate engagement preserves accountability and ends access according to the agreement.'),
E('Choose the right framework or legal source','Frameworks answer different questions. CSF organizes risk-management outcomes; ISO/IEC 27001 addresses an information-security management system; control catalogs and CIS safeguards support implementation; ATT&CK describes adversary behavior; D3FEND organizes countermeasures; CWE describes weakness types; CVSS expresses vulnerability severity. SSDF and SLSA address development and build integrity.<p>Use the Standards desk to compare these with cloud, privacy, and sector guidance. The law index links primary sources across jurisdictions. Applicability, commencement, amendments, and facts matter. India’s IPC entry is marked historical alongside BNS rather than copied as a current blanket rule. Consult qualified institutional support for a real compliance or disclosure decision.</p>','A framework mapping is not a legal conclusion or evidence that a control operates. Check the exact source, revision, and context.','For a fictional project, choose one outcome framework, one implementation baseline, and one evidence artifact. Explain why each is appropriate.','Which pairing is accurate?',['CWE describes weakness types; CVSS describes severity scoring','ATT&CK grants legal permission to test','A CSF checklist automatically certifies ISO compliance'],0,'These references serve different purposes and should not be treated as interchangeable.'),
E('Read research and build a progression plan','Research begins with a narrow question, a model of assumptions, related work, an evaluation method, and a statement of limitations. Use the Research index to find IEEE S&P, USENIX Security, ACM CCS, NDSS, and relevant journals. A prestigious venue is a route to evidence, not a reason to accept every claim without reading the method.<p>For a final-year project, choose a reproducible study with synthetic or approved data: detection false positives, log normalization, access-policy review, or secure-build provenance. Progress from foundations to technical depth, scoped assessment, and professional reporting. Avoid memorizing tool commands without understanding protocols. Keep credentials in proportion to your goals and verify provider status before planning an exam.</p>','A credible portfolio or research result states what was tested, what was learned, what could be reproduced, and what remains uncertain.','Read one accessible paper. Write its question, assumptions, dataset, evaluation, one limitation, and a safe replication idea for your project.','What should a student replication proposal include?',['A question, authorized data, a measurable method, and limitations','Only a tool screenshot','A promise that the method works in every environment'],0,'A bounded, reproducible evaluation is more credible than an unsupported general claim.') ]}
];
modules.push(...additions);
